@yongruilin @rakyll since we're removing this interface, there's a related clean-up worth doing here. 

Today, handlers tell the reconciler what action to take by mutating task.Status.Phase in memory before calling Reconcile. The reconciler then checks that value to decide whether to suspend or resume the actor. As noted in #515, this mixes intent with observed state.

Since Server will call TaskReconciler directly, can we make the action explicit? For example, exposing separate Resume and Suspend methods on TaskReconciler rather than passing everything through a single Reconcile call. That also makes the "new tasks start suspended" behavior an explicit step in CreateTask rather than an implicit side effect of the phase field.

@rakyll Here's a proto sketch, named `TrafficPolicy`: in Substrate, "gateway" is the proxy that enforces these rules (agent-substrate/substrate#3258 is about swapping it), and `NetworkPolicy` collides with the Kubernetes NetworkPolicy Substrate puts on worker pools. Let me know if it makes sense?

```proto
message TaskSpec {
  // ...
  // traffic_policy binds the TrafficPolicy whose rules allow the task's
  // network traffic. A task without one has no network access.
  TrafficPolicyRef traffic_policy = 8;
}

message TrafficPolicyRef {
  string name = 1;
}

// --- TrafficPolicy ---

// TrafficPolicy declares which traffic may reach and leave the Tasks that bind
// it. Both directions are allowlists: traffic that matches no rule is denied.
message TrafficPolicy {
  string api_version = 1;
  string kind = 2;
  ObjectMeta metadata = 3;
  TrafficPolicySpec spec = 4;
}

message TrafficPolicySpec {
  // ingress must have no rules until Agent Substrate can enforce them per
  // actor (agent-substrate/substrate#431). Until then, `ax apply` rejects
  // non-empty ingress.rules and the router's cluster-wide allowlist decides
  // who may call a task.
  Ingress ingress = 1;
  Egress egress = 2;
}

// Ingress lists who may call the tasks that bind the policy.
message Ingress {
  repeated IngressRule rules = 1;
}

// IngressRule lets callers whose SPIFFE ID is listed in from reach the listed
// ports of the task.
message IngressRule {
  IngressPeer from = 1;
  Ports ports = 2;
}

message IngressPeer {
  repeated string spiffe_ids = 1;
}

// Egress is copied into the egress policy of a bound task's actor on Agent
// Substrate when the task is created; later edits or deletes of the policy
// don't change existing tasks. Its rules are an unordered set: when several
// match a destination, the most specific hostname decides (an exact name, then
// "*.domain", then "*"), and no two rules may match the same hostname pattern
// on the same port.
message Egress {
  repeated EgressRule rules = 1;
}

// EgressRule sets exactly one of http, https, and tls_passthrough.
message EgressRule {
  HttpRule http = 1;
  HttpsRule https = 2;
  TlsPassthroughRule tls_passthrough = 3;
}

// HttpRule allows cleartext HTTP to hostnames.
message HttpRule {
  repeated string hostnames = 1;
  // ports defaults to 80.
  Ports ports = 2;
}

// HttpsRule allows HTTPS to hostnames. The egress gateway terminates TLS to
// inspect each request, so the task must trust the gateway's CA; AX provides
// it to tasks whose policy has an https rule.
message HttpsRule {
  repeated string hostnames = 1;
  // ports defaults to 443.
  Ports ports = 2;
  // credentials are added by the egress gateway, so secrets never enter the
  // sandbox. They are not allowed on a rule with the hostname "*".
  repeated CredentialHeader credentials = 3;
}

// TlsPassthroughRule allows TLS to hostnames, forwarded without decryption.
message TlsPassthroughRule {
  repeated string hostnames = 1;
  // ports is required.
  Ports ports = 2;
}

message Ports {
  repeated int32 numbers = 1;
}

// CredentialHeader replaces the value of a request header the task sends, such
// as `x-api-key: placeholder`, with prefix followed by the secret's value.
// Requests without the header are forwarded unchanged.
message CredentialHeader {
  string header = 1;
  string prefix = 2;
  // secret_key is a key of a Kubernetes Secret in the namespace named after
  // the policy's atespace, as for Model.spec.secret_key.
  SecretKeyRef secret_key = 3;
}
```

- RPCs and `ax apply/get/describe/delete trafficpolicy` mirror `Model`; creating a task that binds a missing policy fails.
- First PR ("Part of #549") ships egress + credential injection (`ate-secret://k8s.io/...`) and rejects non-empty `ingress.rules` until agent-substrate/substrate#431 lands.
- Follow-ups: `TaskSpec.model` for automatic Model rules (Q1), plus setup-scoped egress for `Workspace`.

@yjjnls thanks for checking this again and for the walkthrough, and sorry for the silence. The "PR to follow" stalled because only collaborators can open pull requests here.

I've rebuilt the fix on current main (0484e74) as one commit: https://github.com/mastersingh24/ax/commit/2ce9782 (branch `fix-ready-update-time-r2`). It takes your second point into account. Ready's time now always comes from something that only changes when an input does:

- while the task is being deleted, the task's `metadata.deleteTime` (this covers all three terminating branches);
- otherwise the actor's `metadata.updateTime`, or WorkspaceReady's time for a running task when that is later;
- when the actor is gone, or Substrate returned it without an update time, the task's own `metadata.updateTime` (then `createTime`).

On current main a Substrate error no longer sets Ready at all, so that window is already gone. Because nothing falls back to the current time any more, if Substrate ever stopped setting `update_time` the timestamp would get coarser (the task's own time) but would still not change between reads; there's a test for that case. The fake Substrate server now sets `update_time` on actor writes, as the real one does.

There's a table test for each source that reads twice from a fresh stored task, and a server test that reads an unchanged task through GetTask and ListTasks. Both fail on main without the change. `make verify` is clean.

If this looks right, could a maintainer open the PR from that branch, as with #551?
